   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# wisdom of the crowd"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "860c64c8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The \"wisdom of the crowd\" is a phenomenon observed in decision-making, where the collective opinion or judgment of a group of individuals tends to be more accurate or insightful than the judgment of any single member of the group, even those who may be experts in the field. This concept suggests that the aggregated opinions or guesses of a diverse group of people can often outperform individual experts or algorithms in certain contexts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6cc3210e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Approaches to ensemble learning"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0d640f91",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Voting: In voting, multiple models are trained independently, and then their predictions are combined through a majority voting scheme (for classification tasks) or averaging (for regression tasks). This is effective when the models are diverse and make different errors, as the ensemble can cancel out individual errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating): Bagging involves training multiple instances of the same model on different subsets of the training data. Each model is trained independently, and the final prediction is made by averaging (for regression) or voting (for classification) over the predictions of all models. Random Forest is a popular example of a bagging algorithm, where decision trees are trained on random subsets of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Boosting: Boosting is an ensemble technique where models are trained sequentially, and each subsequent model focuses on the examples that previous models found difficult to classify correctly. The final prediction is usually a weighted sum of the predictions of all models. Gradient Boosting Machines (GBM) and AdaBoost are well-known boosting algorithms.\n",
    "\n",
    "Stacking (Stacked Generalization): Stacking combines multiple models through a meta-model or a stacking algorithm. Instead of simply averaging or voting over the predictions of base models, stacking uses another model (the meta-model) to learn how to best combine the predictions of the base models. Base models are trained on the original data, and their predictions serve as features for training the meta-model. This technique often leads to improved performance compared to individual models."
   ]
  },
  {
